.TH CCOL_SELECT 3 "2026-07-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_select \- wait for readiness on any of several queues or file descriptors (blocking)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t ccol_select(size_t *ready_index, size_t n,
                        ccol_selectable *selectables);

ccol_retval_t ccol_select_timed(size_t *ready_index, size_t n,
                        ccol_selectable *selectables, int timeout_ms);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Blocks the calling thread until at least one of the n selectables becomes
ready, then writes its 0-based index into *ready_index and returns.

ccol_select() never performs the receive or send itself, for any
selectable type: it only reports readiness.  The caller must, immediately
afterward, perform its own explicit non-blocking receive or send on the
winning selectable: circq_try_recv_zc()/dynmq_try_recv_zc() or
circq_try_send_zc()/dynmq_try_send_zc() for a queue selectable,
read(2)/recv(2) or write(2)/send(2) on selectables[*ready_index].fd for an
fd selectable.  A concurrent consumer or producer may win the race between
ccol_select() returning and that explicit call (the same TOCTOU contract
POSIX select(2) itself has), so the call must be non-blocking and its
result checked.

ccol_select_timed() accepts an additional timeout_ms argument.  Passing -1
produces the same behaviour as ccol_select() (wait indefinitely).  Passing 0
performs a non-blocking poll.  A positive value is the maximum number of
milliseconds to wait before returning ccol_timed_out.  The deadline is
computed once before the loop using CLOCK_MONOTONIC so that system time
adjustments cannot extend the timeout.

ccol_select() is equivalent to ccol_select_timed(ready_index, n,
selectables, -1).

A ccol_selectable wraps a circular_queue, a dynamic_queue, or a raw file
descriptor.  Build one with selectable_from_circq(), selectable_from_dynq(),
selectable_from_chan(), or selectable_from_fd().  Channels are pre-resolved
to their underlying circular queue before being passed here; ccol_select()
treats them identically to plain circular queues.

When at least one fd selectable is present, both functions create an
epoll(7) instance internally (EPOLL_CLOEXEC) and watch both user file
descriptors and per-waiter eventfd(2) descriptors used to bridge queue
notifications into the epoll event loop.  User fds are registered once
before the main retry loop with level-triggered semantics (EPOLLIN or
EPOLLOUT) and remain registered for the entire call; closing the epoll
instance on return removes them automatically.  Per-queue eventfds are
created on the first registration and reused across iterations (drained
with a non-blocking read in Phase 3 instead of closed), so subsequent
iterations incur zero epoll_ctl or eventfd syscalls.  When no fd
selectables are present, the lighter condvar path is used and no epoll
instance is created.

One waiter node per selectable is heap-allocated at the start of the call
and freed on every return path.  There is no fixed cap on the number of
concurrent threads calling ccol_select() on the same queue.

Locking protocol
Each loop iteration has three phases:

Phase 1 (scan + register)
    For each selectable, acquire the queue's mutex and peek (never
    consume/reserve): read direction checks for at least one message;
    write direction checks for room.  If ready, forward the notify to the
    next waiter in the queue's own waiter list (so a thread that is about
    to stop watching still gives the next waiter a chance to observe the
    same still-true condition) and return.  If not ready, prepend a
    heap-allocated waiter node to the queue's internal waiter list, then
    release the mutex.

Phase 2 (wait)
    Condvar path (no fd selectables): lock the local sel_mtx and call
    pthread_cond_wait() (or pthread_cond_timedwait() when a deadline is
    set) on the local CLOCK_MONOTONIC sel_cond.  The ready flag is
    checked in a while loop to absorb spurious wakeups and signals that
    arrived between Phase 1 and the actual sleep.  Producers set the
    ready flag under sel_mtx before signalling sel_cond, so no wakeup
    is ever lost.  On ETIMEDOUT with ready still false, ccol_timed_out
    is returned.  If pthread_cond_timedwait() itself reports any other
    error, ccol_unexpected_failure is returned rather than retrying.
    Epoll path (fd selectables present): call epoll_wait() with the
    remaining timeout recomputed on each attempt (including EINTR
    retries) so signal delivery cannot extend the deadline.  n_ready==0
    means the deadline elapsed; ccol_timed_out is returned.

Phase 3 (deregister)
    Re-acquire each queue's mutex, splice the waiter node out of the
    list, and loop back to Phase 1 to find which queue is ready.

Lock ordering: producers acquire (queue mutex, then sel_mtx).  ccol_select
holds each queue mutex alone in Phases 1 and 3, and sel_mtx alone in
Phase 2.  No two mutexes are ever held simultaneously by ccol_select, so
there is no lock-ordering cycle and therefore no deadlock.

Node lifetime: producer notification happens while the producer holds the
queue mutex.  Deregistration also requires the queue mutex.  A node
therefore cannot be removed from the list while a producer is traversing
it; the heap buffer backing the node array cannot be freed mid-traversal.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
ready_index   Pointer to a size_t that receives the 0-based index of the
              selectable that became ready.  Valid only when ccol_success
              is returned.
n             Number of elements in the selectables array.  Must be >= 1
              and no larger than INT_MAX, rejected with ccol_invalid_args
              otherwise (see RETURN VALUE); also, separately, no larger
              than SIZE_MAX / sizeof(internal waiter node).  No realistic
              caller can reach either ceiling.
selectables   Array of n ccol_selectable values to monitor.
timeout_ms    (ccol_select_timed only) Maximum milliseconds to wait.
              -1: infinite wait (same as ccol_select).  0: non-blocking
              poll.  Positive: wait up to that many milliseconds.  Any
              other negative value is also treated as an infinite wait,
              identically to -1.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success            A selectable is ready and *ready_index is set.
                        The caller must perform its own explicit receive
                        or send afterward (see DESCRIPTION).

ccol_timed_out          (ccol_select_timed only) timeout_ms elapsed
                        before any selectable became ready.
                        *ready_index is unmodified.

ccol_invalid_args       ready_index or selectables is NULL; n is 0,
                        larger than INT_MAX (a matched selectable's own
                        index is narrowed through a plain int
                        internally), or larger than SIZE_MAX / sizeof
                        (internal waiter node) (the internal
                        per-selectable waiter-node array would overflow
                        size_t); unreachable with any n a caller could
                        realistically construct; a queue selectable
                        contains a NULL queue pointer; an fd selectable
                        contains a negative fd; or an unrecognised type
                        tag is present.
ccol_not_enough_memory  malloc failed to allocate the internal waiter node
                        array (one node per selectable).
ccol_unexpected_failure epoll_create1(2), eventfd(2), or epoll_ctl(2)
                        failed with an unexpected errno.  Reachable for
                        both functions, but only when at least one fd
                        selectable is present.  For ccol_select_timed()
                        specifically, also returned when no fd
                        selectable is present and timeout_ms is
                        non-negative, if the internal timed
                        condition-variable wait itself fails with an
                        error other than a timeout.  Treat as a fatal
                        configuration error (e.g., fd-table exhaustion
                        or memory pressure).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Waiter nodes are heap-allocated (one per selectable) for the duration of
the call and freed on every return path, including error paths.  This
eliminates the VLA stack-overflow risk for large n.

Any number of threads may simultaneously call ccol_select() watching the
same queue.  The waiter list is a dynamically-linked list with no fixed
capacity.

When a queue becomes ready while watched by multiple ccol_select()
threads, only the head waiter is woken by the producer (single-wake).
The waiter that wakes forwards the notify to the next waiter in line
before returning, unconditionally (not contingent on whether its own
subsequent explicit receive/send succeeds), so every interested waiter
eventually gets a chance to observe the same readiness (cascading wake).
This prevents both thundering herd and liveness gaps when multiple
messages arrive, or multiple slots free up, in quick succession.

When writing is disabled on a queue (via circq_disable_sending() or
dynmq_disable_sending()), read-direction waiters on that queue remain
registered and continue waiting.  ccol_select() will unblock once sending
is re-enabled and a message arrives.  Write-direction waiters are woken
immediately so they can observe the new state.

File descriptors must be kept open for the entire duration of the
ccol_select() call.  Closing an fd that ccol_select() is currently
watching produces undefined behaviour.

Likewise, a queue or channel passed to selectable_from_circq()/
selectable_from_dynq()/selectable_from_chan() must not be destroyed
while a ccol_select()/ccol_select_timed() call is still watching it:
circular_queue_destroy()/dynamic_queue_destroy()/channel_destroy() assert
if the queue still has a linked waiter (from either an in-progress
ccol_select()/ccol_select_timed() call or a live event_loop
registration), since the still-linked waiter node would otherwise
reference the queue's own, about-to-be-freed mutex.

The same fd may appear in more than one selectable within a single call,
in any mix of directions (including an outright duplicate of the same
direction twice); for example, watching one connected socket for both
readability and writability at once.  Selectables sharing an fd are
combined into a single underlying registration internally; when it fires,
ccol_select() resolves the returned index to whichever one of the sharing
selectables, in the order they were supplied, has its own direction
actually satisfied by what became ready.

Uses POSIX mutexes and condition variables internally; epoll(7) and
eventfd(2) when fd selectables are present (Linux only).
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
/* Fan-in from two circular queues and a channel: */
size_t idx;
ccol_retval_t r = ccol_select(&idx, 3,
    (ccol_selectable[]){
        selectable_from_circq(q0, ccol_select_read),
        selectable_from_circq(q1, ccol_select_read),
        selectable_from_chan(ch, ccol_select_read),
    });
if (r == ccol_success) {
    printf("selectable %zu is ready\en", idx);
}

/* Equivalent using the convenience macro: */
r = ccol_select_va(&idx,
        selectable_from_circq(q0, ccol_select_read),
        selectable_from_dynq(dq, ccol_select_read),
        selectable_from_chan(ch, ccol_select_read));

/* Mixed read+write: wake when q0 has room OR dq has a message: */
r = ccol_select_va(&idx,
        selectable_from_circq(q0, ccol_select_write),
        selectable_from_dynq(dq,  ccol_select_read));

/* Fan-in from a queue and a pipe read end: */
int pfd[2];
pipe(pfd);
r = ccol_select_va(&idx,
        selectable_from_circq(q0, ccol_select_read),
        selectable_from_fd(pfd[0], ccol_select_read));
if (r == ccol_success) {
    c_message_t msg;
    if (idx == 0) {
        /* queue is ready; claim it ourselves (zero-copy) */
        if (circq_try_recv_zc(q0, &msg) == ccol_success) {
            printf("queue message size: %zu\en", msg.size);
            free(msg.data);
        }
    } else {
        /* fd is ready; read it ourselves */
        char buf[512];
        ssize_t n = read(pfd[0], buf, sizeof(buf));
        printf("read %zd bytes from fd\en", n);
    }
}

/* Timed wait with 500 ms deadline: */
r = ccol_select_timed_va(&idx, 500,
        selectable_from_circq(q0, ccol_select_read));
if (r == ccol_timed_out) {
    printf("no source ready within 500 ms\en");
}

/* Watch one connected socket for either direction, whichever comes first: */
r = ccol_select_va(&idx,
        selectable_from_fd(sock_fd, ccol_select_read),
        selectable_from_fd(sock_fd, ccol_select_write));
if (r == ccol_success) {
    if (idx == 0) {
        char buf[512];
        ssize_t n = read(sock_fd, buf, sizeof(buf));
        printf("read %zd bytes\en", n);
    } else {
        write(sock_fd, "hi", 2);
    }
}
.fi
